Date: Wed, 1 Dec 93 13:25:45 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: BIO: Two noteworthy items...

> From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)

> [It's an interesting idea, but the writer of this article has probably
> "softened" the science here to the point of being almost unrecognizable.
> I'd like to see Calvin's own words. --EI]

That reminds me: a few months ago, there was a mention here of an
April '92 NYT article describing a technique for checking
mathematical proofs, by means of which one can verify a proof by
checking only a few spots.  I recently ran into the actual material
referenced, and it appears that the newspaper article is quite
confused.

The paper referenced, Arora+Lund+Motwani+Sudan+Szegedy 1992, proved
that a particular class of problems (MAX-SNP-complete, including
well-known things like vertex covering) can be approximately solved
(have a polynomial-time approximation scheme) only if P=NP, which is
generally presumed false.  This theorem is mentioned in the
article.  

In the development of this theorem, they apparently used a technique
by which a witness string to a particular statement is polynomially
expanded in such a way that any error has a chance of showing up in
any bit.  This allows you to perform a probabilistic check using a
number of tests only logarithmic in the 1/error desired.  This trick
is also mentioned in the article, e.g. in the headline.  However,
the MAX-SNP-complete theorem is a real theorem, not a probabilistic
result.  I don't know the details of this saga, because I haven't
read the paper.

What's interesting is that the NYT picked up on this result as a
means of easily testing proofs.  This seems highly unlikely.  If a
proof is correctly translated into formal notation, it could
probably be transformed into a form suitable to the application of
this result.  But this probabilistic test can only gain you speed.
If a proof is properly formalized so that it can be applied, the
proof could be checked step-by-step as well.  This rigorous method
would be preferable to most mathematicians.  The problem, of course,
is the usual problem with formal correctness proofs: the formality.
If one overlooks an error in thinking about a step in a proof, one
will very likely write the inaccurate-but-errorless step when
translating to a formal notation.

