Date: Wed, 1 Dec 1993 22:06:30 -0500
From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Random Thoughts

>Please elaborate, long description is appreciated.
>What criteria are to be used to decide whether you would like a book or not?

It is an application of something that I think used to be called
multidimensional voting theory, back when I brushed against it long ago. 

Consider an n-dimensional space, representing characteristics of books. No
assumptions are made initially about what characteristics, or even what
sort of characteristics, each dimension represents. A reader's ideal book
is a point somewhere in the space. A book is a point somewhere in the
space. 

Your customers tell you what books they have liked or disliked, and how
much. Your computer program constructs a set of reader ideal points and
book locations that fits that information. You look for books that a reader
has not read that appear to be located close to his ideal point and
recommend them to him.

Each time a reader reports on another book, you use the information to
improve your estimate of the location of the reader, using your estimate of
the location of the book based on other readers' reports, and you use it to
improve your estimate of the location of the book, using your estimate of
the location of the reader based on his past reports on other books.

As the process continues, you get a more and more accurate map of the
location of books and readers. When a new reader signs up, you send him a
questionaire about books he has read in the past and use his answers to
locate his ideal point. When a new book is published, you wait until enough
of your readers have reported on it to give you a good idea of its
location.

In practice, things will not be this simple. You will have to do lots of
experiments to see what the smallest n is that describes readers'
preferences reasonably adequately. And it may turn out that a reader (or a
book?) is described not by a single point but by several separate points,
or perhaps a surface in the n-dimensional characteristic space.

Once you have the mapping more or less worked out, you can try to figure
out what it means--what characteristics the dimensions represent. If you
can do that, you can then make a first aproximation guess for the location
of a new book by reading it, and refine your estimate as the reports come
in, instead of starting with no information at all.

On another topic ... . Another of my ideas is the video game exerciser. The
advantage of getting your exercise by a sport, such as tennis or sword and
shield fighting, is that while you are doing it you are too busy returning
the serve or blocking a blow to notice how tired you feel. The disadvantage
is that it generally requires at least one other person, equipment, space,
... and that a sport cannot easily be tailored to provide just the exercise
you need, in the way in which an exercise regime on exercise equipment can
be.

The solution is a cross between an exercise machine and a video game. The
controls are moved by your hands and feet (and perhaps legs, and head, and
... . The controls have real resistance to being moved. You are too busy
trying to shoot down the alien saucer that is shooting at your space ship
to notice how tired the arm that is pushing the thruster control is. In the
second generation version, the machine monitors things like heartbeat and
adjusts the play of the game and the resistence of the controls
accordingly. When my heartbeat drops below my aerobic zone, a little figure
in a space suit tumbles on screen calling "help me, help me," and I have to
spin my ship (not noticing how tired my legs on the gyro controls are) and
fire up the thruster ... bringing my hearbeat back up.

