Does anyone have any references/cites to work being done in this area
(adaptive classification schemes)?  This is something that I'm very
intrigued by...I have hopes of someday using a netnews-like system
that uses similar techniques to help me filter the "interesting"
articles in as natural a way as possible.

I was recently involved in a conversation about this very topic, in
which we split the problem into two domains: first, the classification
of archived material, such as the books already published and in the
Library of Congress.  Second, the problems involved with trying to
adaptively classify *new* material, such as a constant stream of news
articles.  The classification system itself would be very useful, and
is certainly a whole area of study in itself, but there's a
bootstrapping problem (alluded to by David) in waiting for enough
ratings/feedback to appear to reliably classify new information.  Some
kind of pattern-matching AI system would probably come in very handy
here.  It would watch the evolving characteristic space and generate
heuristics for making the "first guess" that David talked about - if
it were very important to you not to allow any algorithmic lossage,
you could weight the AI's opinion very low....

Another interesting possibility for the system we were talking about
(for rating netnews) was the ability to weight ratings (human or
machine generated) in accordance to how close the rater comes to you
in the "rater space"....  Of course, that might tend to generate
serious "peaks" (or attractors) in the rater space, which might cause
some undesirable effects on the users.

Various algorithms for this kind of system are pretty easy to come up
with - is anyone actually *doing* this kind of work?  I'd love to see
the database of a few sample systems after several months of usage -
the ideas sound really good, but I'm curious as to how serious the
backfire potential is....

As for the other topic:

   In the second generation version, the machine monitors things like heartbeat
   and adjusts the play of the game and the resistence of the controls
   accordingly. When my heartbeat drops below my aerobic zone, a little figure
   in a space suit tumbles on screen calling "help me, help me," and I have to
   spin my ship (not noticing how tired my legs on the gyro controls are) and
   fire up the thruster ... bringing my hearbeat back up.

You'd also probably want to take into account the adrenalin rush
generated by the distressed astronaut...  As a sometimes-avid vidgame
player, I know from experience the human body can get itself pretty
pumped up trying to react to all the images floating by....

